#Acronyms in SOC

#AC – Alerts count, total count of alerts received
  #Overall load of SOC analysts

#FPR – False positive rate/total alerts
  #Level of noise in the alerts

#AER – Escalated Alerts/Total Alerts
  #Experience of L1 analysts

#TDR – Threat Detection Rate/Total Threats
  #Reliability of the SOC team

#SLA – Service Level Agreement
  #Document signed by the internal SOC team and its company management, or by the managed SOC provider (MSSP) and its customers

#MTTD – Mean Time To Detect
  #Average time between the attack and its detection by SOC tools

#MTTA – Mean Time to Acknowledge
  #Average time for L1 analysts to start triage of the new alert

#MTTR – Mean Time To Respond
  #Average time taken by SOC to actually stop the breach from spreading

#Notes

#5-30 alerts per day per L1 analyst is a good metric

#False positive rates should never be higher than 80%, and cannot realistically hit 0%, though that is the ideal

#Alert escalation rate should realistically be at 50%, but ideally best below 20%

#Threat detection rate should always be at 100%, since anything less can have devastating consequences

